  "worked_solution": "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion with light damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+10y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-3t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is a linear polynomial. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a linear polynomial, i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha +\\beta t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =-3,$ $\\beta =5.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-3+5t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3t}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+5t-3.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $P$ and $Q$ gives\n\n$$\nP=3,\\,Q=4.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-3t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+5t-3.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\pi $ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & ={e}^{-3\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )+5\\pi -3 \\\\  & =(-3{e}^{-3\\pi }+5\\pi -3)\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
